/* book Worked example: Q: Find x(t) for X(s) = (3s + 5)/(s² + 4s + 3) when the ROC is (a) Re(s) > −1, (b) −3 < Re(s) < −1, (c) Re(s) < −3.
   1. Degrees: numerator 1, denominator 2, so X(s) is proper; no division.
   2. Factor: s² + 4s + 3 = (s + 1)(s + 3). Poles at s = −1 and s = −3.
   3. Write X(s) = A/(s + 1) + B/(s + 3).
   4. Cover-up for A: A = (3s + 5)/(s + 3) at s = −1 = (−3 + 5)/(−1 + 3) = 2/2 = 1.
   5. Cover-up for B: B = (3s + 5)/(s + 1) at s = −3 = (−9 + 5)/(−3 + 1) = (−4)/(−2) = 2.
   6. Check by recombining: 1·(s + 3) + 2·(s + 1) = 3s + 5. So X(s) = 1/(s + 1) + 2/(s + 3).
   7. (a) Re(s) > −1 is to the right of both poles: x(t) = (e−t + 2e−3t)u(t), a right-sided signal.
   8. (b) −3 < Re(s) < −1 is to the left of −1 and to the right of −3: x(t) = −e−tu(−t) + 2e−3tu(t), a two-sided signal.
   9. (c) Re(s) < −3 is to the left of both poles: x(t) = −(e−t + 2e−3t)u(−t), a left-sided signal.
   Answer: A = 1, B = 2. (a) (e−t + 2e−3t)u(t); (b) −e−tu(−t) + 2e−3tu(t); (c) −(e−t + 2e−3t)u(−t). Only (a) contains the jω axis. */
