/* book exam traps: Calling a system stable because all denominator coefficients are positive. (s + 1)(s² + 1) = s³ + s² + s + 1 has poles on the jω axis. | Forgetting the ROC: H(s) = 1/(s − 1) is unstable if causal, but stable (and non-causal) with ROC Re(s) < 1. | Calling a pole on the jω axis 'stable' because the impulse response stays bounded. BIBO stability needs ∫|h(t)|dt to be finite; a simple pole at s = 0 makes a step input give a ramp. | Writing H(s) = Y(s)/X(s) from an equation with nonzero initial conditions. H(s) is defined at initial rest; the initial conditions belong to the zero-input response. | Treating zeros as modes. Zeros do not create terms in h(t); they change the coefficients of the modes that the poles create. */
